      "At around eighty, Moses encountered God at a burning bush on Mount Horeb, who commissioned him to return to Egypt and demand Pharaoh release the Israelites, revealing the divine name YHWH ('I AM WHO I AM') in the process. Moses protested his inadequacy — his uncertain standing, his speech difficulties — repeatedly, and God provided his brother Aaron as a spokesman (Exodus 3:1-4:17).",
      "Moses and Aaron confronted Pharaoh, whose refusals to release the Israelites triggered ten devastating plagues on Egypt, culminating in the death of every Egyptian firstborn son while Israelite households were spared by the blood of a sacrificed lamb on their doorposts — the origin of Passover (Exodus 7-12). Pharaoh finally relented, then pursued the departing Israelites to the Red Sea, where Moses, at God's command, parted the waters for Israel to cross and then closed them over the pursuing Egyptian army (Exodus 14).",
      "In the wilderness, Moses led a people prone to complaint and rebellion — over food, water, and eventually the golden calf idol they built while he was on Mount Sinai receiving the Ten Commandments and the broader body of Israelite law over a forty-day period (Exodus 16-17; 32; 34:28). His intercession repeatedly stayed God's anger against the people, most dramatically after the golden calf incident, when he pleaded for Israel's survival at the risk of his own standing (Exodus 32:9-14, 31-32).",
