      "That fame proved dangerous. King Saul's jealousy escalated into open pursuit, and David spent years as a fugitive in the wilderness of Judah, gathering a band of loyal men, twice sparing Saul's life when he had clear opportunity to kill him, and forging a deep friendship with Saul's son Jonathan that the text repeatedly emphasizes (1 Samuel 18-26). David briefly took refuge among Israel's enemies, the Philistines of Gath, before Saul's death at Gilboa opened the way for his return.",
      "After Saul's death, David was anointed king first over Judah at Hebron, and seven years later over all Israel, uniting the fractured tribes (2 Samuel 2:4; 5:3). He captured the Jebusite stronghold of Jerusalem and made it his capital, and brought the Ark of the Covenant into the city with public celebration, establishing it as both the political and spiritual center of the kingdom (2 Samuel 5:6-9; 6:12-19). God's covenant with David, promising that his house and kingdom would endure forever (2 Samuel 7:12-16), became the theological foundation for later messianic expectation.",
      "David's military campaigns extended Israel's borders further than at any prior point, subduing the Philistines, Moabites, Ammonites, Edomites, and Arameans and consolidating a regional kingdom (2 Samuel 8). But his reign's turning point came with his affair with Bathsheba, the wife of his soldier Uriah the Hittite: David had Uriah killed in battle to cover the resulting pregnancy, prompting the prophet Nathan's confrontation and parable, and David's stark confession, 'I have sinned against the Lord' (2 Samuel 11-12).",
