    lifeStory: [
      "Tiberius Julius Caesar was the stepson of Augustus, born to Augustus's wife Livia from her first marriage, and was eventually adopted by Augustus and designated his successor after the deaths of Augustus's preferred heirs. He became emperor upon Augustus's death in AD 14 and reigned until his own death in AD 37, making him the emperor who ruled through nearly the entirety of Jesus's adult life and ministry.",
      "Luke 3:1-2 dates the beginning of John the Baptist's ministry precisely: 'In the fifteenth year of the reign of Tiberius Caesar... the word of God came to John.' Depending on whether this is reckoned from Tiberius's sole accession in AD 14 or from an earlier co-regency with Augustus that began around AD 11-12, this places the start of John's ministry around AD 28-29 — a key anchor point for dating the broader Gospel chronology, including the start of Jesus's own public ministry shortly afterward.",
      "Tiberius's reign grew increasingly withdrawn and unpopular in its later years; ancient historians describe him retreating to the island of Capri after AD 26 and governing largely through subordinates, notably the prefect Sejanus until Sejanus's fall and execution in AD 31. It was during Tiberius's reign that Pontius Pilate governed Judea and presided over Jesus's crucifixion, a fact the Roman historian Tacitus records explicitly when discussing the persecution of Christians decades later under Nero.",
    ],
    occupation: "Second Roman emperor, AD 14-37",
